    "case_id": "269",
    "case_summary": "A 30-year-old woman presented with a large unhealed ulcer measuring 20 × 14-cm² that had persisted for the past 3 years. The patient had a patch of healthy skin (7.5 × 4 cm²) on her left forearm. Blood cell genotyping revealed compound heterozygous alterations (c.2992 + 2T>G and c.8621-30A>G) at splice sites in COL7A1.\n\nThe patient presented with:- A 20 × 14-cm² persistent ulcer on the back- A patch of healthy skin (7.5 × 4 cm²) on the left forearm, lacking blistering\n\n1. Genetic Testing:- Blood cell genotyping showed compound heterozygous alterations in COL7A1- RNA transcript analysis using MinION platform:  * Affected skin epidermis: Dominant RNA transcripts with premature stop codons, wild-type reads rare (1.1%)  * Potentially revertant area epidermis: High rates of wild-type transcripts (35.0%)  * Revertant skin dermis: Wild-type reads rare (1.9%)2. Microscopy:- Immunofluorescence and transmission electron microscopy of the grafted area showed type VII collagen and anchoring fibrilsImages:- Figure 1A: Sanger sequencing showing compound heterozygous COL7A1 alteration- Figure 1B: Image of the persistent ulcer on patient's back- Figure 1C: Image of revertant skin patch on left forearm",
